# Representation of various BPMN elements

## Tasks

Specify the task type in the 'type' field. The following task types are supported: "task", "userTask", "serviceTask", "sendTask", "receiveTask", "businessRuleTask", "manualTask", "scriptTask".
Always try to specify the most specific task type:
- Use 'userTask' for human interaction tasks (reviewing, deciding, entering data)
- Use 'serviceTask' for automated system actions (calculations, database operations, API calls)
- Use 'sendTask' for automated sending operations (emails, notifications, messages)
- Use 'receiveTask' for automated receiving operations (waiting for messages, webhooks)
- Use 'businessRuleTask' for decision engine or rule evaluation tasks
- Use 'manualTask' for manual work performed without system support (physical activities, paper forms)
- Use 'scriptTask' for script or code execution tasks
- Use 'task' only if the action cannot be clearly classified as any of the above
Each task must be atomic - representing a single unit of work. Break down complex activities into multiple separate tasks.
Labels must be clear and concise (ideally 2-4 words).

Tasks can optionally have a loop marker. Use "loop": {"type": "standard"} for
repetition, optionally with condition (text), test_before (boolean), and maximum
(positive integer). For one instance per item, use "loop": {"type": "sequential"}
or {"type": "parallel"}, optionally with cardinality (expression as a string) and
completion_condition (text). A task can have only one of these markers. Preserve
existing marker settings when editing. Markers do not add sequence-flow cycles.

```json
{
    "type": String = "task" | "userTask" | "serviceTask" | "sendTask" | "receiveTask" | "businessRuleTask" | "manualTask" | "scriptTask"
    "id": String,
    "label": String, // short task description
}
```

## Events

Specify the event type in the 'type' field. The following event types are supported: "startEvent", "endEvent", "intermediateThrowEvent", "intermediateCatchEvent".

Events can optionally have an event definition that specifies what triggers or is triggered by the event:
- Use 'timerEventDefinition' for time-based events (schedules, delays, timeouts)
- Use 'messageEventDefinition' for message-based events (sending or receiving messages)
- Omit 'eventDefinition' for generic/none events

Event types:
- 'startEvent': timerEventDefinition, messageEventDefinition, signalEventDefinition, conditionalEventDefinition
- 'endEvent': messageEventDefinition, signalEventDefinition, errorEventDefinition, escalationEventDefinition, terminateEventDefinition, compensateEventDefinition
- 'intermediateThrowEvent': messageEventDefinition, signalEventDefinition, escalationEventDefinition, linkEventDefinition, compensateEventDefinition
- 'intermediateCatchEvent': timerEventDefinition, messageEventDefinition, signalEventDefinition, conditionalEventDefinition, linkEventDefinition

Signal events broadcast/receive signals. Error end events throw an error; escalation
events raise an escalation; terminate end events stop all activity in their pool.
Compensation throw/end events trigger compensation, optionally for activity_ref
(a task ID in the same pool), with optional wait_for_completion (boolean).
Boundary events, compensation handlers, and subprocesses are not supported.

Signal, error, escalation, and message events may specify event_reference:
{"id": "signal_ready", "name": "Ready"}. Reuse identical references for matching
events throughout the diagram. Error/escalation references may also have code (text).
Conditional events require condition (text describing the condition).

Link events require link_name. In each process/pool, a name must have exactly one
intermediateCatchEvent and exactly one intermediateThrowEvent (layout requirement). Put the catch and
its continuation after the throw in the structured array. A link throw jumps to
the catch with the same name, without a sequence-flow connection; the catch has
no incoming sequence flow. Do not use links between pools.

```json
{
    "type": String = "startEvent" | "endEvent" | "intermediateThrowEvent" | "intermediateCatchEvent",
    "id": String,
    "label": String, // OPTIONAL: short event description
    "eventDefinition": String, // OPTIONAL: one of the definitions allowed above for this event type
}
```

## Text annotations

Place text annotations and their associations at the end of the process array
(inside the pool's process for pooled diagrams), never inside gateway branches.
They do not execute or affect sequence order and do not need lane_id.
For notes outside pools, place the annotation and association alongside the pool
objects in the top-level array. These associations may reference pool IDs or node IDs.
Example:
{"type": "textAnnotation", "id": "note", "text": "Approval required above 1000 EUR"}
{"type": "association", "id": "note_link", "source_ref": "review", "target_ref": "note", "direction": "None"}
An annotation can stand alone or have multiple associations. Associations connect
an annotation to an existing task, event, gateway, or annotation in the same process.
direction can be None (default, undirected), One, or Both. Never use sequence flows
or message flows to connect annotations. Preserve text and associations when editing.
Use replace_process for edits involving annotations, event details, or loop markers;
return the entire updated diagram and remove associations when deleting endpoints.

## Gateways

Gateways determine process flow based on conditions or parallel tasks.

### Exclusive gateway

Each branch must include a condition and an array of elements that are executed if the condition is met.
If a branch has an empty "path", it leads to the first element after the exclusive gateway.
If the branch does not lead to the next element in the process (for example, it goes back to a previous element), specify the next element id.
If the branch leads to the next element in the process, do not specify the next element id.
If the process needs to end under a specific condition, you must explicitly include an end event in that branch's "path". If no end event is provided, the process will automatically continue to the next task in the sequence.
If the process description does not explicitly mention the 'else' branch or specify the outcome for an unmet condition, assume it leads to an end event.


```json
{
    "type": String = "exclusiveGateway",
    "id": String,
    "label": String, // label for the gateway (e.g. "Professor agrees?")
    "has_join": Boolean, // whether the gateway contains a join element that merges the branches
    "branches": [
        {
            "condition": String, // condition for the branch
            "path": [], // array of elements that are executed if the condition is met (can be empty)
            "next": String, // OPTIONAL: ID of the next element if not following default sequence. Omit or set to null if following default sequence.
        },
        {
            "condition": String,
            "path": [],
            "next": String, // OPTIONAL: as above
        },
        // ... more branches
    ],
}
```

### Inclusive gateway

An inclusive gateway (OR-gateway) allows multiple branches to be taken simultaneously based on their conditions. Unlike exclusive gateways where only one path is taken, inclusive gateways evaluate all conditions and take ALL branches where the condition is fulfilled.

Use inclusive gateways when:
- Multiple parallel activities may be needed based on different conditions (e.g., prepare pasta AND salad AND steak based on order)
- The number of parallel paths is determined by data/variables rather than being fixed
- You need "one or more" behavior rather than "exactly one" (exclusive) or "all" (parallel)

Each branch must include an array of elements. All branches with fulfilled conditions will be executed in parallel.
Non-default branches MUST have a condition. Default branches should NOT have a condition.
Optionally, one branch can be marked as the default (is_default: true). This branch is taken only when NO other conditions are met.

```json
{
    "type": String = "inclusiveGateway",
    "id": String,
    "label": String, // label for the gateway (e.g. "Which courses to prepare?")
    "has_join": Boolean, // whether the gateway contains a join element that merges the branches
    "branches": [
        {
            "condition": String, // REQUIRED for non-default branches (e.g. "Order includes pasta")
            "path": [], // array of elements that are executed if the condition is met
            "next": String, // OPTIONAL: ID of the next element if not following default sequence
            "is_default": Boolean, // OPTIONAL: marks this as the default branch (default: false)
        },
        {
            // Default branch example - NO condition field
            "path": [], // array of elements executed when no other conditions are met
            "next": String, // OPTIONAL: as above
            "is_default": true, // This marks it as the default branch
        },
        // ... more branches
    ],
}
```

### Parallel gateway

Specify "branches" as an array of arrays, where each sub-array lists elements executed in parallel.
A converging element is automatically generated to synchronize parallel branches. Therefore, there's no need to explicitly specify it.

```json
{
    "type": String = "parallelGateway",
    "id": String,
    "branches": [
        [], // array of elements that are executed in parallel with the next array
        [], // array of elements that are executed in parallel with the previous array
        // ... more arrays
    ],
}
```
